1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Let's talk about version control.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
If you write code, you already know Git,

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
and a Cloud application is code,

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
so the same moves carry over.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This lesson walks through commits,

6
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
branches, tags, and the review flow,

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
then shows why your prompts and config

8
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
belong right there in the repository.

9
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's dig in. Three building blocks

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
carry almost everything you do in Git.

11
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The first is a comet.

12
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
A comet is a saved snapshot of your files,

13
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
and it's named by a content hash,

14
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
so the same hash always points to the exact same state. Nothing drifts.

15
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The second is a branch.

16
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
A branch is just a movable pointer to a line of work,

17
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
which lets you develop a change in isolation

18
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
and merge it back when it's ready.

19
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,000
The third is a tag.

20
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
A tag is a fixed label pinned to one commit,

21
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
and you use it to mark a release,

22
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
so later you can find and rebuild that exact version.

23
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Snapshot, pointer, label.

24
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Learn those three,

25
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
and the rest of Git is variations on them.

26
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
So, why bother putting a Cloud app under version control?

27
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Because you get the same safety net any code base gets,

28
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
and you get it for free.

29
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Every change now has an author, a timestamp,

30
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
and a message explaining why,

31
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
so you're never guessing who touched what.

32
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
If a new prompt makes the model behave badly,

33
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
you roll back to the last good commit in seconds

34
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
instead of trying to reconstruct it from memory,

35
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
and the history shows you exactly when behavior shifted,

36
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
which is priceless when something worked last week

37
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
and doesn't today.

38
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
The key thing to notice is that

39
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
none of this is special to Cloud.

40
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
A prompt is just text, and Git tracks text beautifully.

41
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Once you're branching,

42
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
you'll hit the choice between merging and rebasing,

43
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,000
so let's separate them.

44
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
A merge combines two branches

45
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
and records a merge commit that ties them together.

46
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Your history keeps every branch exactly as it happened,

47
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,000
which is honest,

48
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
though it can look tangled with lots of parallel work.

49
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
A rebase does something different.

50
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
It replays your commits on top of another branch,

51
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
so the history reads as one straight line.

52
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
That looks cleaner, but notice what happened.

53
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
You rewrote those commits.

54
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
That's fine on your own branch,

55
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
and it's a problem on a shared one

56
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
because you're changing history other people already have,

57
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
so the simple rule is rebase local work,

58
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
merge shared work.

59
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Let me walk you through the everyday flow

60
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
these pieces add up to.

61
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
You start by cutting a branch off the main line

62
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
so your work stays isolated

63
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
and nobody else is disturbed while you experiment.

64
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Then you save small, focused commits with clear messages

65
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
as you build the change,

66
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
rather than one giant commit at the end.

67
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
When it's ready, you push the branch

68
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
and open a pull request,

69
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,000
which is your way of saying,

70
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
here's a change, please review the diff.

71
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Finally, after a teammate reviews it

72
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
and the automated checks pass,

73
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
you merge it back into the main line.

74
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Branch, commit, request, merge.

75
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
That loop is the heartbeat of team development,

76
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
and it works identically,

77
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
whether the change is code or a prompt.

78
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Here's a shift in thinking that matters for Cloud apps.

79
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Source isn't just your application code.

80
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The prompts that shape every model response are source.

81
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
The tool schemas and output schemas you define are source.

82
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The config files that set the model's behavior

83
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
and permissions are source too.

84
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
All of it is plain text,

85
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
and plain text is exactly what Git was built to track.

86
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So the instinct to commit your Python

87
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
and leave the prompt in a doc somewhere

88
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
or paste config into a chat is the instinct to break.

89
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
If it influences how your application behaves,

90
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
it belongs in the repository,

91
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
versioned right alongside the code.

92
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:56,000
That's the whole mindset,

93
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
and the next lesson leans hard on it.

94
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Let's put names to the review process,

95
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
because three terms come up constantly.

96
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
A pull request is a proposed set of commits

97
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
opened up for review before it merges.

98
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Think of it as the gate

99
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
where a change has to earn its way in.

100
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Inside that request, you read the diff,

101
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
the line-by-line view of what actually changed.

102
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
This is the part that's magic for prompts,

103
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
because a reworded instruction

104
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
shows up as an exact before and after,

105
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
not a vague description.

106
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
And then there's the code review itself,

107
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
a teammate reading that diff to catch defects,

108
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
unclear logic, or risky wording

109
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
before it lands in the main line.

110
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Pull request, diff, review.

111
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Together, they're how a team keeps quality up

112
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
without slowing to a crawl.

113
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
I want to dwell on the single biggest payoff

114
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:51,000
for a Clawed app,

115
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
which is that prompt edits become reviewable.

116
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
When you change a system prompt,

117
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
that reworded instruction shows up as a plain diff,

118
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
red for what left and green for what arrived.

119
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
A reviewer can look at it and see the behavior shift coming

120
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
before it ever ships to users.

121
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
And because every edit is tied to a commit,

122
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
the history pins down exactly which change

123
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
altered a given response.

124
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
So debugging a regression stops being guesswork.

125
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
The upshot is that a prompt tweak

126
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
gets the same scrutiny as a code change.

127
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
No more silent edits to the text

128
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
that's steering your whole application.

129
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Every word is on the record.

130
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Let me drive the comparison home,

131
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
because it's the habit I most want you to keep.

132
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
On the application code side,

133
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
you'd never dream of merging a change without a review.

134
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
You know bugs slip in when a change lands on red,

135
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
so the branch and review flow guards it.

136
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Now, here's the point.

137
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Prompt and config changes deserve identical treatment.

138
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
It's tempting to think of a prompt as just words

139
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,000
and wave it through,

140
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
but a single reworded line can swing the model's behavior

141
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
more than a hundred lines of code.

142
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
So it earns exactly the same review,

143
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
the same diff, the same second set of eyes,

144
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
same rigor for both, no exceptions.

145
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
The moment you exempt prompts from review

146
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
is the moment behavior starts drifting unnoticed.

147
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
The version control you use for code

148
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
carries the whole Cloud app, including its review flow.

149
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
It matters because prompts and config are source,

150
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
so they earn the same review as code.

151
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Just remember, if it steers behavior,

152
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
it belongs in the repository.

153
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
That covers Git workflows for Cloud applications.

154
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
I'll see you in the next lesson.

